(chapter
  (slug "whats-next")
  (title "Where to go next")
  (pillar "language")
  (section "Wrapping up")
  (blurb "A recap, one last program, and how to keep exploring.")
  (lede "You've met the core of Cadenza. Here's what you can now read, write, and reason about.")
  (h2 "What you've learned")
  (p "You can bind values and write functions (" (link (slug "basics") "Values &amp; functions") "), pipe and compose them (" (link (slug "functions") "Composing functions") "), branch and recurse (" (link (slug "control-flow") "Control flow") "), compare and order (" (link (slug "ordering") "Comparison &amp; ordering") "), and bundle data into tuples and records, reading, nesting, and functionally updating it (" (link (slug "data") "Tuples &amp; records") ", " (link (slug "records-tuples") "Working with records &amp; tuples") "). You decide by shape with " (link (slug "pattern-matching") "pattern matching") ", and you've met the collections, namely " (link (slug "lists") "lists") ", " (link (slug "maps-sets") "maps &amp; sets") ", and lazy " (link (slug "iterators") "iterators &amp; ranges") ", along with the text and binary types, " (link (slug "strings") "strings") " (down to the individual " (c "Char") "s that compose them), " (link (slug "bytes") "bytes") " (and " (link (slug "binary-matching") "binary matching") " to build and destructure them), and " (link (slug "symbols") "symbols") ". You handle " (link (slug "errors") "absence and errors") " with " (c "Option") "/" (c "Result") ", and you know how Cadenza's numbers behave, from checked " (link (slug "numbers") "integers") ", " (link (slug "sized-integers") "sized integers") ", " (link (slug "floats") "floating-point numbers") ", and " (link (slug "rationals") "exact fractions") ", none of them ever converting silently.")
  (p "And you've seen what makes Cadenza its own language: " (link (slug "effects") "effects &amp; handlers") ", where a handler decides what a performed operation means; " (link (slug "contracts") "design by contract") ", where " (c "@requires") " and " (c "@ensures") " turn a function's assumptions and promises into checks enforced at its boundary; " (link (slug "modules") "modules") " that are just records of their exports; " (link (slug "opaque-types") "opaque types") " that hide a representation behind a module boundary so an invariant can't be broken; " (link (slug "units") "units of measure") " that catch a dimensional mistake at compile time and then vanish before the program runs; " (link (slug "types-as-values") "types as values") " you can reflect, compare, and branch on at compile time; " (link (slug "ad-hoc-polymorphism") "ad-hoc polymorphism") " where one operator name dispatches on operand type and a generic function specializes per type, all resolved at compile time; " (link (slug "const-parameters") "const parameters") " that fix an argument at compile time, whether a constant, a type, or a dictionary, inlined into a specialized copy and then erased; " (link (slug "metaprogramming") "metaprogramming") " where a program is an ordinary AST value you can quote, take apart, build, and eval; " (link (slug "property-testing") "testing") ", where " (c "@test") " marks a function as a test and parameters make it generative, so the runner synthesizes the inputs and shrinks any failure, no testing DSL required.")
  (p "More importantly, you've seen the ideas underneath, the " (link (slug "philosophy") "tenets") " that explain " (em "why") " the language makes the choices it does. Determinism and capability-safety holding for every program. Declining rather than miscompiling. One program, many syntaxes. Refusing the ambiguous conversion. These aren't trivia; they're the through-line, and they'll make the rest of Cadenza feel predictable.")
  (h2 "One last program")
  (p "A little of everything: a recursive helper, a comparison, and a record, computing the larger coordinate of a point, doubled.")
  (runnable
    (source "(def (max a b) (if (> a b) a b))
(def (main)
  (let ((p #record((= x 3) (= y 8))))
    (* 2 (max (. p x) (. p y)))))"))
  (p "Edit it, changing the point or the factor, or swapping " (c "max") " for " (c "min") ", and Run. Then flip the syntax toggle in the header and watch the very same program re-appear in the other surface.")
  (h2 "Keep exploring")
  (p "The best way forward is to keep an example open and change it. Every code block in this guide is a scratchpad, so break it, fix it, and press Run. That loop, with the compiler answering instantly, is the fastest way to build a feel for the language.")
  (p "When you outgrow a single code block, move to the " (link (slug "playground") "playground") ": a full editor with a REPL for calling your functions one at a time, a peek at the WebAssembly and Rust your code compiles to, and a Share button that packs a whole program into a link.")
  (p "And to see the language doing real work, browse the " (link (slug "example-apps") "example applications") ": the calculator, CAD preview, notebook, and playground, each a differentiator you learned running as a full program.")
  (p "Finally, if you want to see where the language leads, there's a second pillar to this guide: " (link (slug "platform-overview") "Cadenza the Platform") ". It's the agent kernel Cadenza was built to run on, where an agent's whole state is a pure fold over an event log and the language is what flows through it, so the effects, sum types, and total functions you just met become the way a real agent records its history and replays it. It's a concept tour rather than more language to learn, so read it when you're curious how these ideas add up to a platform."))
